WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:03.970
[whispering] I'm inside one of the
Inchindown oil tanks,

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:07.720
an old oil storage facility
in Inverness, in Scotland.

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:12.720
This tank is 237 metres long
and it's not meant for humans.

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:15.010
When it was in use by the Royal Navy

00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:19.590
all this colossal space
would have been filled with fuel oil.

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:22.540
With these oil tanks, the Royal Navy
accidentally created

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:25.920
the greatest reverberation chamber
in the world.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:29.560
Flat surfaces
enclosed by solid rock,

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:33.400
and a chamber big enough that
sound takes a whole second or more

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.940
to go off the far wall
and bounce back.

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:40.650
But still narrow enough
that you get lots of reverberation

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:43.240
from all the close surfaces as well.

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:45.140
- The construction started in 1938

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:48.010
and it carried on right through
the beginning of the war

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:50.360
up to around about 1943.

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:55.970
The Admiralty had decided that they
required bomb-proof storage for their oil.

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:59.730
So they set about building a number
of oil tanks across Britain.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:02.260
And this, Inchindown, is one of them.

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:06.140
The construction required
six tanks to be built.

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:10.900
The entire complex could easily contain
100,000 tonnes of fuel

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:15.890
but one single Royal Navy battleship
can drink 5,000 tonnes.

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:18.090
So it could go very, very quickly.

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:22.100
It is a truly amazing chamber of sound,

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:24.993
a cathedral of oil and
a cathedral of sound.

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:29.470
- So now I'm going to shut up for a bit.

00:01:29.470 --> 00:01:31.710
Preston here is going to
fire a starting pistol,

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:33.850
a very, very loud bang.

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:36.840
On the screen is a visual
display of that sound.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:39.360
Different frequencies will reverberate
for different amounts of time

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:41.747
so you will see that and hear that.

00:01:41.747 --> 00:01:44.140
And as it gets quieter
we will turn up the level

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:47.510
on the microphone so it's
easier for you to hear.

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:49.197
Right, everyone ready?

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:09.680
Thank you to the Bannerman Group
for letting us into the tunnels,

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:13.050
to Allan, Alan and Preston
for helping with everything here,

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:14.720
to Matt Gray who is behind the camera

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:18.420
and who's had some musical
experiences over on his channel.

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:19.253
Go for it.

00:03:20.277 --> 00:03:22.610
Aagh!

00:03:26.573 --> 00:03:27.699
That is terrifying.

